# File contexts for the wlsctx module, in CIL form.
#
# build.sh appends this to build/wlsctx.cil (after the `checkmodule -C`
# rules), so `semodule -i build/wlsctx.cil` installs the rules AND these
# file contexts in one shot, with the label living *in the module* (removed
# again by `semodule -r wlsctx`).
#
# Why CIL filecons instead of a .fc: on policycoreutils 3.11 the module
# store is CIL-based, and its install path reads file contexts straight
# out of the module CIL (libsemanage calls cil_filecons_to_string after
# cil_compile and writes them to the policy's file_contexts — see
# libsemanage/src/direct_api.c). A .fc only reaches the store through the
# .pp, whose gen_context form the CIL writer serializes unresolved and the
# reader rejects (LESSONS #25). Inline CIL filecons avoid that entirely.
#
# Format is the CIL `filecon` statement — byte-for-byte what `semodule
# export` emits (the store re-parses its own CIL, so this round-trips):
#   (filecon "PATH" FILETYPE (USER ROLE TYPE ((LOW) (HIGH))))
# The context is the standard object context system_u:object_r:wlsctx_exec_t
# with a single s0 level ((s0) (s0) == :s0). The MCS category is applied to
# the *process* at runtime (the self-narrowing re-exec), never to this file,
# so the file stays plain s0. Keep in sync with wlsctx.fc / wlsctx.fc.resolved.
(filecon "/usr/local/bin/wlsctx" file (system_u object_r wlsctx_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))
(filecon "/usr/bin/wlsctx"      file (system_u object_r wlsctx_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))
